"""Compose current control law with explicit RDMS-01 R1 replacement scope."""
from render_layer import DATA, fca, header, imports, message, retained_prose, rule, segment, source_link, write
from schema_selection import schemas

parts = [header("L1", "L1 owns the authoritative control persistence boundary and its deterministic replay representation. Semantic ownership of stored cognitive, action, temporal, memory and recovery entities remains with their defining layer.", "CONTROL LAWS SOURCE-VERIFIED; EVENT/AGGREGATE SERIALIZATION QUESTIONS ISOLATED")]
parts.append(imports([
    ("Constitutional authority and content trust", "L0", "Enforce domain-scoped authority and non-bypassable frozen law."),
    ("Executive authorization and canonical cognitive entity mutations", "L2", "Commit owner-authorized changes, without independently deciding cognition."),
    ("Observation ingress, ActionRecord and Action Journal", "L3", "Serialize raw observation append under its legal authority; retain world-effect reconciliation boundary."),
    ("Trigger reactions, specialists and dependency freshness", "L4", "Atomically persist jointly required metadata and consult the sole freshness engine."),
    ("Memory promotion and revision", "L5", "Require Executive/LearningGate governance and durable artifact identity."),
    ("Recovery admission, suspension integrity and replay safety", "L6", "Use scoped lawful recovery transitions and the recovery barrier."),
    ("Transport delivery of control requests", "L7", "Receiving admission verifies source-owned authority; delivery is not a commit."),
]))
rules = [
    (1, "One committed ControlTransaction is authoritative. Current canonical entity state and append-only replay/control history become authoritative atomically. Neither representation creates a competing legal truth source; full event sourcing is not required.", [1, 2]),
    (2, "Every successful ControlCommit validates, allocates commit_seq, applies entity mutations, appends replay mutations and semantic events, advances ProjectHead and commits in one transaction. All these recoverability-critical writes become durable together or none do.", [2]),
    (3, "ProjectHead records the legal project head: current committed sequence, Executive epoch, state schema and durable project status. It coordinates serialization, fencing, recovery and schema checks; it is not a cognitive memory.", [3]),
    (4, "commit_seq is strictly increasing and unique per project. Physical contiguity is not required. Replay selects commits later than the snapshot's through_commit_seq in ascending legal order; it does not require every integer N+1 to exist. Event ordinal orders representation within an atomic commit, not external-world chronology.", [4, 11]),
    (5, "Use the current ControlCommit and AuthorityContext source declarations below. EXECUTIVE applies to cognitive changes, promotion, action authorization and specialist integration; OBSERVATION_INGRESS admits raw observations; RUNTIME_SYSTEM and RECOVERY authorize only architecturally permitted deterministic/reconciliation transitions. Authority kind alone grants no power: CommitGate validates transition eligibility and the applicable authority reference/epoch.", [5, 6]),
    (6, "The proposer/initiator, semantic authorizer and runtime writer are separate roles. Do not infer authorization from initiator identity. The runtime records initiator_ref and authority_context and writes only the authorized transition.", [7]),
    (7, "Every canonical mutation has a deterministic replay representation. CREATE, REPLACE and TOMBSTONE use expected ABSENT or an expected entity version, a new entity version, payload schema and the committed after-image or payload reference. After-image semantics are the Runtime v1 preference; recovery replays committed results without invoking the LLM, capability or original engineering reasoning.", [8, 9]),
    (8, "Semantic ControlEvents carry meaning, audit and lifecycle history; CanonicalMutations carry replay state. They are distinct and immutable after commitment. Preserve FCA's mandatory canonical-event metadata requirement: event_id, schema_version and idempotency_key. The relationship to the R1 ControlEvent fields is the explicitly isolated CE-01 question below; no metadata deletion or guessed serializer is authorized.", [10]),
    (9, "IDs are opaque, stable, typed, non-reusable and globally unique within the required domain. Type identities must not be mixed. UUIDv7 is only an implementation candidate, not a frozen ID algorithm.", [12]),
    (10, "Mutable canonical entities carry entity_version, created_commit_seq and updated_commit_seq. Creation requires expected ABSENT and new_version 1; update requires the current version and increments it by one. Blind overwrite is illegal. Runtime v1 conflicts are whole-entity conflicts; automatic field-level merging is forbidden. Re-read/reconcile outside the transaction and issue a new proposal.", [13, 14]),
    (11, "WRITE_CONFLICT means the intended write target changed; DEPENDENCY_STALE means a reasoning dependency changed, even if that dependency is not being written. A successful write compare-and-set does not prove dependency freshness. Freshness decisions use the L4 owner contract.", [15]),
    (12, "A redeliverable canonical command carries idempotency_key and request_fingerprint. The recorded identity scope is (project_id, operation_scope, idempotency_key), associated with commit_id and fingerprint. Same key/fingerprint returns the original result; different fingerprint yields IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT. Canonical mutation keys have no automatic TTL.", [16]),
    (13, "Keep control_schema_version, event_type_version, payload_schema_version and snapshot_codec_version distinct. Immutable historical events may be deterministically decoded/upcast but never rewritten; migration preserves their historical meaning and deterministic replay.", [17]),
    (14, "Normal restart may read intact current state directly when its applied marker matches ProjectHead. A snapshot accelerates rebuilding; it is not a truth prerequisite. Rebuild loads a valid snapshot and replays later CanonicalMutations. Snapshot hashes validate actual encoded payload bytes; a semantic hash requires a separately defined canonical serialization.", [18, 19]),
    (15, "CanonicalStore.applied_through_commit_seq equals ProjectHead.latest_commit_seq in the intact state. A lower applied marker requires missing-commit replay. A greater marker claims history absent from the authoritative head and is CORRUPTION/FAILED_SAFE; do not normalize it into an accepted head.", [20]),
    (16, "Large artifacts are outside the ledger, but a reference cannot be committed before bytes are durable and their hash verified. Preserve PREPARED, REFERENCED, ORPHAN and RETIRED artifact lifecycle semantics. RECOVERY_CRITICAL content is not garbage-collected while canonical state or replay history depends on it. Changed bytes require a new artifact identity/hash.", [21]),
    (17, "Indexes and caches are disposable, non-authoritative derived state. IndexMetadata records generation, the source fingerprint and available repo/commit frontier, with BUILDING/CURRENT/STALE/FAILED status. An index cannot independently establish canonical truth or negative-query freshness.", [22]),
    (18, "A dependency RepoRef denotes an identifiable COMMIT, IMMUTABLE_WORKING_SNAPSHOT or SANDBOX_SNAPSHOT, with revision and content fingerprint. A mutable filesystem path is not an immutable repository reference. WorldRef represents only the operation-relevant world/environment dimensions, not an omniscient world snapshot.", [23]),
    (19, "Durable ProjectStatus is ACTIVE/SUSPENDED/ARCHIVED/FAILED_SAFE. ProcessRuntimeMode is INITIALIZING/RECOVERING/READY/DEGRADED/STOPPING and is process-local. Startup initializes/recoveries anew and becomes READY only after the recovery barrier; READY cannot be treated as a lasting project fact after a process dies.", [24]),
    (20, "Fast recovery opens the authoritative store, reads ProjectHead, checks the applied sequence, acquires/fences the Executive epoch, inspects unresolved actions, replays triggers, validates specialists/dependencies and crosses the RecoveryBarrier before READY. Rebuild reconstructs current state from a valid snapshot plus replay and verifies it against ProjectHead before normal reconciliation. Neither route relies on chat history or re-running reasoning.", [25]),
    (21, "Raw observation append need not receive Executive semantic authorization, but it still enters ControlTransaction and receives legal per-project commit ordering. Concurrent ingress and Executive transitions do not race an independently writable ledger head.", [26]),
    (22, "The storage engine prevents dirty canonical reads, lost updates, double allocation of authority and partial ControlCommit. No named SQL isolation level or particular database engine is frozen. Required storage semantics include ACID control transactions, uniqueness, monotonic per-project ordering, optimistic entity versions, durable acknowledgement, append-only history and crash recovery.", [27]),
]
parts.append("## Effective control requirements\n\nR1 expressly replaces the full-event-sourcing/sole-ledger projection model, gapless numbering, prematurely fixed ID algorithm, actor/authority conflation, prior snapshot/status shape and entity concurrency assumptions. Unchanged project isolation, immutable artifacts, timestamp meaning and external-effect separation remain in force.\n\n")
for n, text, sources in rules:
    src = [segment(1, f"{number}. ") for number in sources]
    if n == 8:
        src.append(fca(48))
    if n in [1, 2]:
        src.extend([fca(48), fca(49)])
    if n == 18:
        src.append(segment(0, "28. "))
    if n == 22:
        src.append(segment(0, "45. "))
    parts.append(rule(f"L1-C{n:02d}", text, src))
parts.append(rule("L1-C23", "Each ProjectRuntime has independent canonical state, Executive epoch, commit ordering, Action Journal and trigger lifecycle even when projects share a physical process or database. Immutable observations, raw episodes and committed events are corrected by new records/relations, not by overwriting captured history. commit_seq defines legal ordering; wall-clock timestamps serve temporal reasoning, latency, timeout and forensic purposes.", [segment(0, "1. "), segment(0, "11. "), segment(0, "26. ")]))
parts.append(rule("L1-C24", "Do not implicitly enlist a filesystem, GitHub, production database or external API in ControlTransaction. Durable local intent precedes external effect; observation and reconciliation establish what happened. A control commit or leadership handoff does not erase dispatched world effects. ActionGate/Action Journal ownership remains L3; recovery admission remains L6.", [segment(0, "37. "), fca(50), fca(51)]))
parts.append(rule("L1-C25", "global_state_version equals the latest legally committed per-project commit_seq, including observation append. It is historical ordering, not semantic freshness; entity_version changes only for that entity. The original uint64 declaration for these counters remains a source constraint; R1 relaxes contiguity, not typed counter meaning.",[segment(0,"5. "),segment(0,"9. "),segment(0,"10. "),segment(1,"4. "),segment(1,"13. ")]))
parts.append(rule("L1-C26", "Preserve correlation_id for task/intent lineage and causation_id for the immediate triggering event/command. Semantic transition classes are COGNITIVE_COMMIT, OBSERVATION_APPEND, CONTROL_LIFECYCLE, MEMORY_PROMOTION and WORLD_ACTION_LIFECYCLE; these are not five databases. Raw observation append validates schema/provenance/integrity and cannot smuggle in interpretation. Learning promotion requires proposal, LearningGate, Executive authorization and traceable lifecycle. Disposable isolated sandbox micro-actions may aggregate under SandboxActivityRecord.",[segment(0,"25. "),segment(0,"30. "),segment(0,"31. "),segment(0,"32. "),segment(0,"33. "),segment(0,"34. "),segment(0,"35. ")]))
parts.append(rule("L1-C27", "After failure, distinguish definitely committed, definitely uncommitted, possibly world-effecting, unresolved and required reconciliation work. If losing one half of control metadata while preserving its required partner would make recovery ambiguous, both share the authoritative transaction. Search/index/cache state cannot override current canonical state or legal committed history.",[segment(0,"36. "),segment(0,"42. "),segment(0,"13. "),segment(1,"1. ")]))
parts.append(retained_prose(segment(0,"2. "),"L1-B02"))
parts.append("## Owned source-defined record surfaces\n\nThese are the exact current R1 declarations and unchanged artifact/world reference declarations. They state source spelling, collections, enums and optionality; they do not imply a production serialization API. Source-independent fields are not added.\n\n")
for name in ["ProjectHead", "ControlCommit", "AuthorityContext", "CanonicalMutation", "ControlEvent", "Snapshot", "IndexMetadata", "RepoRef", "ProjectStatus", "ProcessRuntimeMode", "ArtifactRef", "WorldRef"]:
    entries = [d for m in DATA["messages"][:2] for d in m["declarations"] if d["name"] == name and not d["partial_or_example"]]
    chosen = entries[-1]
    parts.append(f"### {name}\n\n```text\n{chosen['raw']}\n```\n\nSource: {source_link(chosen)}; declaration SHA-256 `{chosen['raw_sha256']}`.\n")
parts.append("## Remaining bounded serialization questions\n\n**CE-01:** FCA §48 requires every canonical event to include `event_id`, `schema_version`, `idempotency_key`; RDMS-01 R1 §10's ControlEvent lists `event_id`, `event_type`, `event_type_version`, `ordinal`, `payload`. The sources do not explicitly equate a ControlEvent with the whole canonical event envelope, bind the inherited key to an enclosing commit, or rename schema_version to event_type_version. Both logical requirements remain mandatory. Their exact record/envelope mapping is not declared complete, and no lossy event serializer is approved.\n\n**PR-01:** RDMS-01 baseline §1's ProjectRuntime aggregate references `control_head` and `runtime_status`; R1 introduces ProjectHead and separates durable project status from process-local mode. Project-scoped authority and all required semantics above are current. A revised literal ProjectRuntime transport/aggregate field shape is unspecified; a schema that preserves the old mixed RuntimeStatus as current law is rejected. This does not block implementation-independent control laws or unrelated owner contracts.\n\n")
parts.append("## Amendment and baseline coverage\n\nAll R1 §§1–28 are represented in the effective rules and record surfaces; §28's RDMS-I01…22 invariants constrain those rules. R1 §29 is a documentary adversarial retest, §30 admits the corrected specification, and §31 is historical phase progression rather than new law. Baseline §§1–47 are reconciled by the scoped replacement ledger; §48 is historical next-phase material. The original baseline ArtifactRef/WorldRef, project isolation, immutability, logical/wall-clock separation and external-transaction prohibition remain effective. Actor identity remains distinct from authority; LedgerHead and mixed RuntimeStatus are not exported as competing current canonical definitions. The L4-owned DependencyStamp is imported, not redefined by its older L1 foundation sketch.\n\n")
parts.append(retained_prose(segment(1,"28. "),"L1-R1-INVARIANTS"))
parts.append(schemas("L1",exclude=("ProjectHead","ControlCommit","AuthorityContext","CanonicalMutation","ControlEvent","Snapshot","IndexMetadata","RepoRef","ProjectStatus","ProcessRuntimeMode","ArtifactRef","WorldRef")))
parts.append("The document's independent control rules and retained record declarations are source-grounded. CE-01/PR-01 prevent asserting a complete event/aggregate mapping or complete layer-wide field reconciliation. Verification must preserve every archived source hash and check these explicit limitations rather than reporting full completion.\n")
write("L1", "\n".join(parts))
print("Rendered architecture/L1_CONTROL.md")
